</div><table>
<thead><tr><th>conversations</th><th>awake call p50 µs</th><th>asleep call p50 / p99 µs</th><th>edit p50 / p99 ms</th><th>restart to first call ms</th><th>records read at restart</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>64</td><td>1.3</td><td>3.5 / 3.9</td><td>2.6 / 4.4</td><td>2.4</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>512</td><td>0.7</td><td>2 / 2.2</td><td>2.7 / 4.7</td><td>2.5</td><td>1</td></tr>
<tr><td>2,048</td><td>0.7</td><td>2.2 / 2.3</td><td>2.7 / 4</td><td>1.9</td><td>1</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="bench-note">Each conversation is one callable actor and four 4,096-byte messages in LMDB with synchronous commits. An asleep call loads the actor's record first. An edit is one call and one durable commit. A restart opens the database in a new Host and answers one call, reading only the record it calls. At 2,048 conversations the Zig heap holds 0.7 KiB per conversation, against 16.8 KiB of records on disk. The heap excludes LMDB's mapped pages, which the operating system caches separately.</p>
```

# [Live sessions]($section.id('live-sessions'))

The capacity run keeps real sessions open while measuring memory. Separate
runs time session opening. These include the driver and sockets, beyond the
core-state sizes in [Per-connection footprint](#per-connection-footprint).

```=html
<!-- bench:sessions GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<p class="bench-stamp">Benchmark snapshot of source <code>v0.1.1-72-gc49b0b78a</code> on 2026-11-05: Linux aarch64, 20 logical CPUs, 122 GiB RAM, Racket v9.3 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0-52-gdcc2b2c5, and the <code>glowcap</code> archives built by Zig 0.17.0 in ReleaseFast. <code>zig build bench-site-report</code> repeats it.</p>
<p class="bench-note">Cleartext loopback TCP with signed CapTP handshakes and a bootstrap fetch on every connection. All sessions remain open at the RSS sample. The server uses 32 HostRuntime shards. A separate raw-peer process supplies load. The run uses an isolated Linux user/network namespace and a descriptor limit of 524288. Linux chooses io_uring or epoll at runtime. RSS excludes kernel socket memory. These are capacity and barrier measurements, not WAN latency or sealed-channel capacity.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>live sessions</th><th>server baseline KiB</th><th>server loaded KiB</th><th>server delta KiB/session</th><th>load process KiB</th><th>barrier seconds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1,000</td><td>11,792</td><td>29,648</td><td>17.86</td><td>25,948</td><td>0.106</td></tr>
<tr><td>10,000</td><td>11,820</td><td>132,320</td><td>12.05</td><td>103,912</td><td>0.417</td></tr>
<tr><td>50,000</td><td>11,780</td><td>458,584</td><td>8.94</td><td>326,484</td><td>1.292</td></tr>
<tr><td>100,000</td><td>11,824</td><td>864,528</td><td>8.53</td><td>602,688</td><td>2.354</td></tr>
<tr><td>500,000</td><td>11,792</td><td>4,178,080</td><td>8.33</td><td>2,803,068</td><td>15.777</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="bench-note">Session opening includes connect, handshake, bootstrap fetch, and close. Each transport runs separately without CPU pinning. The table reports measured latency, without treating signing and handshake primitives as a complete cost model.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>transport</th><th>open p50 µs</th><th>open p99 µs</th><th>open mean µs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>tcp</td><td>341.22</td><td>512.24</td><td>347.67</td></tr>
<tr><td>noise-tcp</td><td>858.32</td><td>1280.08</td><td>867.27</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
```

# Repeat the measurements

`zig build bench-site-report` refreshes the charts from live ReleaseFast runs.
To run one bench, use its name from the section above and add
`-Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.

The [benchmarks README](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/bench/README.md)
also covers primitive costs such as signing, Noise handshakes, and frame
copies, along with calls whose runtimes spin instead of sleeping.
